Synthetic CS module cold-test export
===================================

Provenance
----------
Synthesised. No CS module measurement is included, and none was available to
copy: the shipped `data/CSM` directory is empty and no cold-test CSV has ever
been committed to this repository's history. The file was written to match the
export format `nova/thermalhydralic/cold_test.py` reads, at the smallest size
that still drives every parsing and conditioning branch. Nothing in it is
proprietary, and nothing in it should be cited as a measurement.

Layout
------
The tests redirect the package root, so `data/CSM` below this directory is what
`cold_test` resolves its project directory from, and `test/` is the folder the
reader globs for the test-phase exports.

    CSM_SYNTH/test/coldtest_20200211.csv

The timestamps lie inside the reader's hard-coded `test` window
(2020-02-11 09:00 to 2020-02-14 18:00), because the phase selectors are date
slices rather than parameters.

Export structure
----------------
Seven preamble lines, then a header row, then one row per sample at 10 s
intervals. The reader takes the header from row seven, so the preamble's content
is never parsed -- only its length matters. Each channel name carries its unit in
parentheses, which is how the reader splits a name into a (channel, unit) pair:

    timestamp,PSIOut_(kA),IBus_(kA),mean_TT001_value_(K),TT002_(K),DS001_(mm),DS002_(mm)

`mean_TT001_value_(K)` is written in the instrument's own spelling so the
name-normalising branch runs -- the reader strips `mean`, `value` and underscores
before splitting on the parenthesis. Channels are grouped by prefix first and
unit second, so `DS...` becomes displacement, `TT...` temperature, and anything
in kA current.

Signal content
--------------
The current holds at zero, ramps to 44 kA and back down, and holds at zero
again, at 0.08 kA/s. Both bounds are deliberate: the conditioning discards and
re-interpolates any current sample above 45 kA or any channel whose gradient
exceeds 0.1 per second, so a steeper ramp or a higher peak would be silently
rewritten rather than read.

The two displacement channels follow

    DS001 = -0.5 - k I**2          k = 18 / 44**2 mm/kA**2
    DS002 = -0.5 + 0.5 * DS001

The standoff term keeps the channel strictly negative, which the conditioning
requires of a displacement, and the zero-current hold at the start means the
reader's own offset subtraction removes exactly that standoff. What is left is
the pure square law, so the fitted coefficient is -k with nothing left over --
which is what makes the fit test an equality against a known number rather than
a tolerance against measured data.

Temperatures are constant, in range and smooth, so they survive conditioning and
exercise the grouping without also exercising the rejection branches.
